根据我对 `AGENTS.md` 的阅读，我已识别出分析任务。该文件包含：
1. 一道数学题（三角形排列中关于真值/谎言的学生问题）
2. 一个得出 2031120 的“标准解答”
3. 一份详细的长篇分析，揭示了 XOR/Pascal's-triangle-mod-2 结构

这是一个 Devin 失败分析：该“标准解答”是**错误**的，而详细分析展示了正确的处理方法。以下是我的 XML 分析：

```xml
<analysis>
  <metadata>
    <task_id>full-analysis-30c-r001403-polymath_01072</task_id>
    <task_type>math_problem_analysis</task_type>
    <problem_domain>combinatorial_logic_optimization</problem_domain>
    <date>2026-08-15</date>
  </metadata>

  <problem_summary>
    <description>
      Students arranged in a triangular formation: first row has n students,
      second row n-1, ..., nth row has 1 student. All face the first row.
      Each student (except the leftmost in each row) picks one of two statements
      about the student directly below them and the student to the left of that
      person. Find the maximum number of truth-tellers for n=2015.
    </description>
    <claimed_answer>2031120</claimed_answer>
    <claimed_answer_formula>n(n+1)/2 for n=2015</claimed_answer_formula>
  </problem_summary>

  <failure_identification>
    <verdict>INCORRECT</verdict>
    <error_type>logical_inconsistency_unchecked_assumption</error_type>
    <severity>critical</severity>
    <summary>
      The standard solution assumes ALL students can simultaneously be
      truth-tellers, yielding the trivial total n(n+1)/2 = 2031120.
      This is impossible under the problem's constraints.
    </summary>
  </failure_identification>

  <root_cause_analysis>
    <primary_error>
      <location>Standard solution steps 3, 5, 6, 8</location>
      <description>
        The solution asserts "Row 1: T,T,T,...,T (all true)" and inductively
        claims every row can be all-true, concluding the maximum equals the
        total number of students. It never verifies that the truth assignments
        are CONSISTENT with the statements students make.
      </description>
      <fundamental_mistake>
        Confusing "no one contradicts this" with "this is achievable."
        The solution treats truth values as free choices rather than
        consequences of the statement structure.
      </fundamental_mistake>
    </primary_error>

    <missed_constraint>
      <description>
        When a student at position (r,j) references students at (r-1,j) and
        (r-1,j-1), their truth value is CONSTRAIGNED:
        - If both referenced students have the SAME truth value (both T or both L),
          BOTH statements are false, so the student MUST be a liar.
        - If the referenced students DIFFER, the student CAN choose to be truthful.
        This yields the recurrence: T(r,j) = T(r-1,j) XOR T(r-1,j-1).
      </description>
      <consequence>
        If two adjacent students in a row are both truth-tellers (T=1, T=1),
        the student above them referencing both MUST be a liar (T=0).
        Therefore, "all students truthful" is self-contradictory.
      </consequence>
    </missed_constraint>
  </root_cause_analysis>

  <correct_approach>
    <key_insight>
      The maximizing assignment follows T(r,j) = T(r-1,j) XOR T(r-1,j-1),
      which is Pascal's triangle modulo 2 (Sierpinski triangle structure).
      Boundary values (row 1 and column 1) are free; interior cells are determined.
    </key_insight>

    <formalization>
      <coordinates>
        Row 1 = bottom (first row, n students). Row r has n-r+1 students.
        Position j from left, j = 1,...,n-r+1.
        Non-speaking students: all of row 1, plus position 1 in rows 2..n.
        Speaking students: (r,j) with r>=2, j>=2.
      </coordinates>
      <recurrence>
        T(r,j) = T(r-1,j) XOR T(r-1,j-1) for r>=2, j>=2 (when maximizing).
        If a=b (both 0 or both 1): forced liar, T=0.
        If a!=b: can choose T=1 to maximize.
      </recurrence>
      <free_variables>2n-1 (row 1: n values, column 1: n-1 values)</free_variables>
      <determined_cells>(n-1)(n-2)/2</determined_cells>
    </formalization>

    <small_case_verification>
      <case n="1"><max_truth_tellers>1</max_truth_tellers><total>1</total></case>
      <case n="2"><max_truth_tellers>3</max_truth_tellers><total>3</total></case>
      <case n="3"><max_truth_tellers>5</max_truth_tellers><total>6</total></case>
      <case n="4"><max_truth_tellers>8</max_truth_tellers><total>10</total></case>
      <case n="5"><max_truth_tellers>12</max_truth_tellers><total>15</total></case>
      <observation>
        For n>=3, max < total, disproving the claim that all can be truthful.
        The sequence 1,3,5,8,12 has differences 2,2,3,4 — not yet closed-form.
      </observation>
    </small_case_verification>

    <structural_notes>
      <region_A>
        Cells with j >= r depend only on row-1 variables via Pascal coefficients
        mod 2 (Lucas' theorem: C(r-1,k) odd iff k is a submask of r-1).
      </region_A>
      <region_B>
        Cells with j < r also depend on column-1 boundary variables.
      </region_B>
      <optimization_structure>
        GF(2)-linear optimization: each determined cell is a XOR of boundary
        variables; maximize total Hamming weight of the output vector.
      </optimization_structure>
    </structural_notes>

    <completeness_status>
      The detailed analysis in AGENTS.md does NOT reach a closed-form answer
      for n=2015. It was still enumerating small cases (computing n=6) when
      the analysis terminated. The correct answer for n=2015 remains
      UNRESOLVED in the provided material.
    </completeness_status>
  </correct_approach>

  <error_taxonomy>
    <category name="unchecked_inductive_assumption">
      The inductive step "if row k is all-true, row k+1 can be all-true"
      was never validated against the XOR constraint. It is in fact FALSE:
      two adjacent truth-tellers force the student above to be a liar.
    </category>
    <category name="conflating_free_and_constrained_variables">
      Truth values of speaking students are NOT free — they are determined
      by the statements chosen and the truth values of referenced students.
      The solution treats them as freely assignable.
    </category>
    <category name="missing_consistency_check">
      No verification that the proposed all-true assignment satisfies the
      statement semantics. A single counterexample (e.g., n=3) refutes it.
    </category>
  </error_taxonomy>

  <diagnostic_checks_failed>
    <check id="1">
      Trivial-case validation: for n=3, total=6 but max=5. The all-true
      assignment is impossible. This would have immediately caught the error.
    </check>
    <check id="2">
      Local consistency: pick any two adjacent truth-tellers in a row; the
      student above referencing both must be a liar. Contradicts "all true."
    </check>
    <check id="3">
      Statement semantics: if a=b=1, statement (i) claims b=0 (false) and
      statement (ii) claims a=0 (false). Both false => student must lie.
      This basic case analysis was entirely absent.
    </check>
  </diagnostic_checks_failed>

  <lessons>
    <lesson priority="1">
      For logic puzzles with truth/lie constraints, always derive the
      local consistency condition (here: XOR) before proposing global patterns.
    </lesson>
    <lesson priority="2">
      Verify inductive hypotheses on small cases (n=1,2,3) before extrapolating
      to n=2015. The all-true hypothesis fails at n=3.
    </lesson>
    <lesson priority="3">
      "No contradiction observed" is not "consistency proven." The standard
      solution's step 3 ("no students in front to contradict") confuses absence
      of constraints with absence of contradictions.
    </lesson>
    <lesson priority="4">
      Recognize algebraic structure: XOR recurrences map to Pascal's triangle
      mod 2 / Sierpinski triangle, enabling systematic analysis rather than
      ad-hoc pattern guessing.
    </lesson>
  </lessons>

  <conclusion>
    The claimed answer 2031120 is definitively WRONG. It equals the total
    student count and would require all students to be truth-tellers, which
    violates the XOR constraint T(r,j) = T(r-1,j) XOR T(r-1,j-1). The correct
    maximum is strictly less than n(n+1)/2 for n >= 3, as verified by small
    cases (n=3: 5 < 6, n=5: 12 < 15). The detailed analysis correctly
    identifies the Pascal-mod-2 structure but does not complete the
    closed-form derivation for n=2015 within the provided material.
  </conclusion>
</analysis>
